course: dictionary
lesson: 6273
title: Words with more than one meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. The context, meaning the words and sentences around them, tells you which meaning the writer wants. «Hospital machines monitor heart rate, oxygen and breathing and beep when something needs checking.» Here monitor means to watch and check carefully, not a screen.
explain: «In a job like class monitor, be fair and don't favour your friends.» Now monitor is a pupil with a special job at school. The words class and job are the clues. Always ask: which meaning fits this sentence?
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence to check it. If you read «a heart monitor shows a flat line», you can swap in watch-and-show and it still makes sense. A computer screen would not fit there at all.
fact: Many words have more than one meaning.
fact: «Monitor» can be a noun (a thing or a person) or a verb (an action).
fact: As a noun, a monitor can be a computer screen, a machine that watches and shows something, a school pupil with a special job, or a large lizard.
fact: As a verb, to monitor means to watch or check something carefully over time.
fact: Scientists monitor plant growth, temperature and weather every day and record the results.
exercise: «Hospital machines monitor heart rate, oxygen and breathing.» What does monitor mean here? | To paint a picture | A large lizard | A school job | *To watch and check carefully
exercise: What is a class monitor? | *A pupil with a special job at school | A kind of weather | A baby's cot | A computer screen
exercise: In «parents monitor screen time», what kind of word is monitor? | *A verb | A noun | A question word | An adjective
test: A baby monitor is a machine. What does it do? | Feeds the baby | Makes the room dark | *Watches over a baby and shows what is happening | Sings to the baby
test: «Scientists keep monitoring the temperature.» What are they doing? | Breaking the thermometer | Asking a friend a question | Turning the thermometer off | *Checking it carefully over time
exam: Which meaning of monitor fits «The monitor sat on the rock and flicked its tongue»? | To check carefully | A computer screen | *A large lizard | A pupil with a job
